O:8:"stdClass":8:{s:5:"title";s:89:"UW oceanographers wire up an undersea volcano to study Earth’s most extreme environment";s:4:"link";s:127:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/07/uw-oceanographers-wire-up-an-undersea-volcano-to-study-earths-most-extreme-environment/";s:11:"description";s:701:"<div>Several hundred miles off the coast of Oregon and Washington, there&rsquo;s an undersea volcano known as Axial Seamount.&nbsp;Thanks to a set of high-tech instruments installed last summer by a team of scientists, many from&nbsp;the College of the Environment, the deep sea is online and oceanographers&nbsp;were able to observe an&nbsp;eruption of the Axial Volcano in April 2015. PBS NewsHour&rsquo;s Hari Sreenivasan recently spoke with the School of Oceanography&lsquo;s John Delaney and Debbie&nbsp;Kelley about their team&rsquo;s deep sea observatory, including a network of sensors, moorings, and cameras that collect&nbsp;troves of new information about this underwater world.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-07-07 18:36:06";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}s:7:"src_url";s:127:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/07/uw-oceanographers-wire-up-an-undersea-volcano-to-study-earths-most-extreme-environment/";s:8:"src_name";s:54:"UW oceanographers wire up an undersea volcano to s ...";}